d/id #uuid "60ef36e5-3c06-58f7-856f-d4155cb53598" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473735}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2a75eec7-e20c-53a7-afe2-6cf4faf6becf" :question/math-academy-id "q-131311" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled using a Poisson distribution?\n- **I.** The number of network failures per week\n- **II.** The number of emergency calls received by a hospital every minute\n- **III.** The number of members of a band\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A Poisson distribution models the number of events that occur in an interval of time or space if events occur independently and at a constant average rate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I follows a Poisson distribution. The number of network failures can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know how many events occur in an interval of time (a week).\n\n- Random variable II follows a Poisson distribution.  The number of emergency calls received can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know how many events occur in an interval of time (a minute).\n\n- Random variable III does *not* follow a Poisson distribution. There are no events that occur at a constant average rate, and there is no interval of time or space.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419538 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157308 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f0a57c2-bbad-5225-976b-385c7f117738" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Random Variables that Follow a Poisson Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "904898ac-a1aa-5334-a804-a603ad8c7ee0" :answer