nswer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473528} :answer-field/id #uuid "64d74df4-c3ba-59a9-afc8-fdf98329aaae" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474159}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "71278ae0-44f4-58f6-827b-4b9773ebb613" :question/math-academy-id "q-89222" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled using a Poisson distribution?\n- **I.** The number of emails received by a company's server in one week\n- **II.** The number of people who visit a coffee shop per hour\n- **III.** The height of $5$ buildings in New York City\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A Poisson distribution models the number of events that occur in an interval of time or space if events occur independently and at a constant average rate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I follows a Poisson distribution. The number of emails received by a company's server can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know how many events occur in an interval of time (one week).\n\n- Random variable II follows a Poisson distribution. The number of people who visit a coffee shop can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know how many events occur in an interval of time (one hour).\n\n- Random variable III does *not* follow a Poisson distribution. There are no events that occur at a constant average rate, and there is no interval of time or space.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419541 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157308 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f0a57c2-bbad-5225-976b-385c7f117738" :knowledge-poi