id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472687}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d7d01b4-199a-5d1b-80a4-1f3aef59ace1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472687} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6d9fb68-3ffe-5c1a-bd34-30b76a78e7bf" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473738}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a68d38c1-253b-59e6-ad1e-93208422e4e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-131412" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled using a Poisson distribution?\n- **I.** The average points scored on a math test in a class\n- **II.** The number of orders a firm receives per month\n- **III.** The number of calls a firm will receive next week for help concerning a toy\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A Poisson distribution models the number of events that occur in an interval of time or space if events occur independently and at a constant average rate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I does *not* follow a Poisson distribution. The average points scored on the math test is not an event that occurs at a constant average rate.\n\n- Random variable II follows a Poisson distribution. The number of orders can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know how many events occur in an interval of time (a month).\n\n- Random variable III follows a Poisson distribution. Again, the number of calls can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know how many events occur in an interval of time (a week).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592