We have $9\\in [x]$ if $9∼x.$ Using the definition of the given equivalence relation, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\,|\\,(9-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now consider each of the given equivalence classes.\n\n- $9\\in [5]$ because $2\\,|\\,(9-5)\\,✓$\n\n- $9\\notin [2]$ because $2∤(9-2)\\,\\times $\n\n- $9\\in [19]$ because $2\\,|\\,(9-19)\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421729 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152257 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dacfbca6-6135-5d51-a07e-627fce79dfd1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equivalence Classes Containing a Given Element"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a85e79af-2617-5247-8482-bb788fbf0452" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474882} {:answer/id #uuid "bb77f224-6079-51f9-a77f-252d44a54c3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a85e79af-2617-5247-8482-bb788fbf0452" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474882} :answer-field/id #uuid "074c2cfd-78e9-58e8-8405-666adc0ddf75" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475367} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bf38d88-8706-55c4-8972-3061317da5b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474884} {:answer/id #uuid "b4affa42-c1d2-5675-8171-dcc5f1d1b1dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474885}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4affa42-c1d2-5675-8171-dcc5f1d1b1dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474885} :answer-field/id #uuid "d21f2f2b-49fd-544f-93b3-db53c91c15b0" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475368} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0870d16d-a967-5ca1-8136-ba7ac078415d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474886} {:answer/id #uuid "2b623fe2-18e6-59ca-b7f7-c5a2a514d4e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474887}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b623fe2-18e6-59ca-b7f7-c5a2a514d4e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474887} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5fdd2ab-eb04-5053-89b0-31aab6b55ac1" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475369}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fd41a54f-8627-52be-9ff1-ad5548f86090" :question/math-academy-id "q-77214" :question/problem "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on $Z,$ defined by $x∼y$ if $3\\,|\\,(x-y).$ Which of the following equivalence classes contain the element $5?$\n\nI. $[3]$\n\nII. $[-1]$\n\nIII. $[8]$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "We have $5\\in [x]$ if $5∼x.$ Using the definition of the given equivalence relation, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\,|\\,(5-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now consider each of the given equivalence classes.\n\n- $5\\notin [3]$ because $3∤(5-3)\\,\\times $\n\n- $5\\in [-1]$ because $3\\,|\\,(5-(-1))\\,✓$\n\n- $5\\in [8]$ because $3\\,|\\,(5-8)\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421820 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152291 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6302f81d-4834-56a5-b014-0a97c3dccd6f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About General Solutions of Second-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4049eacb-f333-5928-be15-ad4db6bd4e7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474308} {:answer/id #uuid "35601916-369e-51fe-abf4-791c123c637e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474309}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35601916-369e-51fe-abf4-791c123c637e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474309} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b95782b-8b17-5f77-9801-8a99d11ccf2a" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475080} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b8d5ec7-084e-5c9f-8f80-d0e39dff32c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474310} {:answer/id #uuid "a3e17bc7-fee0-5214-bbe1-e420ad24d8df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474311}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b8d5ec7-084e-5c9f-8f80-d0e39dff32c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474310} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a78c060-c106-55f9-a38d-38117b0a316f" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475081} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45cf437a-d3e6-5bff-a122-cc094f36db9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474312} {:answer/id #uuid "91b92ce5-41cb-5b8f-8a7e-42023c7326d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474313}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45cf437a-d3e6-5bff-a122-cc094f36db9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474312} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc403eaf-5b3a-598a-b3ae-28f5e22f4037" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475082}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "70cf3885-2e2c-5971-b49c-1988c3e0bb2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-130004" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}+2x{y}^{′}-2y=0,$ where $x>0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}_{1}(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ is a solution to the equation\n\nII. ${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{4}$ is a solution to the equation\n\nIII. The general solution of the equation is $y=\\frac{A}{{x}^{2}}+B{x}^{4}$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Let's check each statement:\n\n- To determine whether ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute ${y}_{1}$ and its derivatives into the equation.\n\nFirst, we find the first and second derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}})+2x(-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})-2(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) & =\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}=0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement I is true.\n\n- Similarly, we compute the derivatives of ${y}_{2}$ and substitute to the equation. Computing the derivatives yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =4{x}^{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =12{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}(12{x}^{2})+2x(4{x}^{3})-2{x}^{4}=18{x}^{4}.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}$ is not a solution to the equation, and therefore statement II is false.\n\n- So, ${y}_{1}$ is a solution but ${y}_{2}$ is not a solution of the equation, hence $y=\\frac{A}{{x}^{2}}+B{x}^{4}$ is not the general solution.\n\nTherefore, statement III is false."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421821 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152291 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6302f81d-4834-56a5-b014-0a97c3dccd6f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About General Solutions of Second-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4025a2da-8220-50a7-b4f3-219e6d29cc87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474302} {:answer/id #uuid "91c226a9-4c73-5b94-92b0-1a733df3a834" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474303}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4025a2da-8220-50a7-b4f3-219e6d29cc87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474302} :answer-field/id #uuid "f318ba66-ccb9-5f8e-9231-0f5fb32052aa" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475077} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb83e9ac-cb3a-5dc7-8842-0118c6b9c9ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474304} {:answer/id #uuid "74a023e8-6ba2-5b3f-a47a-189a8481e140" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474305}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74a023e8-6ba2-5b3f-a47a-189a8481e140" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474305} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf5c45ac-f6f4-5fdf-87b6-c55e5d17a5ea" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475078} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab0e9691-308c-596d-9c85-fd9d5dd60a50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474306} {:answer/id #uuid "5c2a6d79-cf64-517d-a658-e230018451ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474307}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab0e9691-308c-596d-9c85-fd9d5dd60a50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474306} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a04d4f0-4217-55ed-ab79-e4478a4fd20e" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475079}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7fd65812-f6ae-584d-bfe8-d11797aed930" :question/math-academy-id "q-130000" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}-4y=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}_{1}(x)=x{e}^{x}$ is a solution to the equation\n\nII. ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}$ is a solution to the equation\n\nIII. The general solution of the equation is $y=Ax{e}^{x}+B{e}^{2x}$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Let's check each statement:\n\n- To determine whether ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute ${y}_{1}$ and its derivatives into the equation.\n\nFirst, we find the first and second derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =(1+x){e}^{x} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =(2+x){e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n(2+x){e}^{x}-4(x{e}^{x})=(2-3x){e}^{x}.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}$ is not a solution to the equation, and therefore statement I is false.\n\n- Similarly, we compute the derivatives of ${y}_{2}$ and substitute to the equation. Computing the derivatives yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =2{e}^{2x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =4{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n4{e}^{2x}-4{e}^{2x}=0.\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement II is true.\n\n- So, ${y}_{1}$ is not a solution while ${y}_{2}$ is a solution of the equation, hence $y=Ax{e}^{x}+B{e}^{2x}$ is not the general solution.\n\nTherefore, statement III is false."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423501 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198384 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58fbdce5-60b9-5682-a84e-1c8a5077c762" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Horizontal Segment Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9148e7a1-c898-5010-a8da-e14468900021" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474442} {:answer/id #uuid "8c509f0c-7869-50fd-97c8-0c39ad974d7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474443}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9148e7a1-c898-5010-a8da-e14468900021" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474442} :answer-field/id #uuid "80fd21d2-d0e2-56a6-83b8-c52c5aa81167" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475147} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "035981b4-173c-59ce-a4cc-935ef26ceb45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474444} {:answer/id #uuid "e09384a8-158b-5092-8870-775a085bfe39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474445}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e09384a8-158b-5092-8870-775a085bfe39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474445} :answer-field/id #uuid "a427db06-c7e8-5666-90dd-909a4adf6847" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475148} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33a494b7-3cef-5f9f-b5df-0019758840e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474446} {:answer/id #uuid "d9805b37-2717-5444-9336-eeda03de0a19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474447}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9805b37-2717-5444-9336-eeda03de0a19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474447} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5f8696b-5d50-5511-a270-5cccd6dc0050" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475149}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fa0b27c3-d4dd-5c0c-8989-66af8729df06" :question/math-academy-id "q-161029" :question/problem "![](images/12/1252844f7a9e2fb2ca0ff1d2e2e1546a460e7d0846b4ae2996b6ce984ee305a1.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\le u\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{5\\pi }{12}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nThe image of the top side of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is a line segment. Which of the following statements are true regarding the image?\n\nI. The image makes the angle of $0$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n\nII. The image makes the angle of $\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n\nIII. The endpoints of the image lie on the circles of radii $\\frac{1}{4}$ and $1$ centered at $O.$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the top side.\n\nFor the top side, we have $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$ and $\\frac{1}{4}\\le u\\le 1.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{12})),\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\le u\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment shown below.\n\n![](images/49/49aa7d6ccd4b14b48ede7486f41ab11ac22b593c06d75795fe7819889b2b2f23.png)\n\n \n\nNote that the line that this segment lies on passes through the origin. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. The slope of the line is\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{12})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{12})}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}).\n$$\n\nAs a result, our line makes an angle of $\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- Statement III is true.\n\n- When $u=\\frac{1}{4},$ we obtain the point \n$(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}),\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{12})),$\nwhose distance to the origin is $\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\n- When $u=1,$ we obtain the point \n$(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{12})),$\nwhose distance to the origin is $1.$\n\nSo, the endpoints of the line segment lie on the circles of radii $\\frac{1}{4}$ and $1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424375 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166841 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "00b14560-29f9-58fc-ac65-5505987579fb" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Situations That Can Be Modeled as a Discrete Uniform Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b14ecc36-432b-56ec-87ab-282eea355b8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475002} {:answer/id #uuid "d834568e-ce97-583c-8040-fc4e266d52c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475003}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b14ecc36-432b-56ec-87ab-282eea355b8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475002} :answer-field/id #uuid "cdad22ae-e385-521b-b788-7347685ca5ee" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475427} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12cca481-ff11-55c7-91de-86d18673b2bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475004} {:answer/id #uuid "e8dfccef-97df-539d-8b11-97c54d234ef3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475005}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "12cca481-ff11-55c7-91de-86d18673b2bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475004} :answer-field/id #uuid "00c49dca-0d5f-5b4f-bddf-bbbd54a75e97" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475428} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09154e84-c363-5ed7-a1c0-e945e77a7a21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475006} {:answer/id #uuid "6cd75d4f-b796-573f-80aa-522e963beda1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475007}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09154e84-c363-5ed7-a1c0-e945e77a7a21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475006} :answer-field/id #uuid "044a1424-5108-5da5-9fee-69fd6070dd31" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475429}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "239e34ce-3c73-5bae-b143-3d41bff8bf51" :question/math-academy-id "q-97754" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution?\n\nI. The temperature of a town on a certain day.\n\nII. The number of letters of a word randomly selected from an English dictionary.\n\nIII. The number of typos in a randomly selected book.\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "We can use the discrete uniform distribution to model the outcome of a single event when the support $S$ is finite, and each $x\\in S$ is equally likely.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because a temperature is a real number, so the support is not finite.\n\n- Random variable II cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. Each outcome is not equally likely (there are more short words than very long words, for example).\n\n- Random variable III cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because the number of typos can be any positive integer. Moreover, each outcome is not equally likely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424377 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166841 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "00b14560-29f9-58fc-ac65-5505987579fb" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Situations That Can Be Modeled as a Discrete Uniform Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2527c71-4b45-56c5-ac1b-b9c3677ec446" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474410} {:answer/id #uuid "538248d4-58af-5ebb-9766-95fbd02d680f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474411}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "538248d4-58af-5ebb-9766-95fbd02d680f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474411} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c6b22b2-16b6-594f-8629-233f8e4533ee" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475131} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "530df43b-d990-5fd4-82d9-edd79acc72b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 