olution "We can use the discrete uniform distribution to model the outcome of a single event when the support $S$ is finite, and each $x\\in S$ is equally likely.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because there are a finite number of integers between $0$ and $10,$ and since the selection is random, each integer has an equal probability of being selected.\n\n- Random variable II cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because there are infinitely many real numbers between $5$ and $9,$ and so the support is not finite.\n\n- Random variable III cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because the number of clients arriving at a shopping mall can be any positive integer, and each outcome is not equally likely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424380 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166841 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "00b14560-29f9-58fc-ac65-5505987579fb" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Situations That Can Be Modeled as a Discrete Uniform Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10973e79-c1b6-57b8-9851-6ae7106d412f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474996} {:answer/id #uuid "b6720f69-c55a-5a21-8a8c-910069c91507" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474997}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6720f69-c55a-5a21-8a8c-910069c91507" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474997} :answer-field/id #uuid "88439eea-1275-53b8-bdc6-f84f85c32054" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id