4265}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7629f35b-fff6-5124-ac59-0a11bbdc38ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474265} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8e5aaaf-be26-5099-8d37-7e8745a6b18f" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475058}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d4fcfc53-7279-50f5-90f1-bd8c9662d8f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-110599" :question/problem "![](images/c3/c36c34462f2ba7f129ba3370d4e67c5ac450b4188be629a256caf7807d4cb2d6.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}$ or ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$  clearly lies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II does not represent a subspace. There are vectors  $a=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$  in the subset for which ${a}_{1}>0$ and ${a}_{2}>0.$   But then\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=[\\begin{aligned}-{a}_{1} \\\\ -{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram III is a line that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426975 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054809 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7fcec0d8-ca03-5446-a42f-65d0ccddd584" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Quadrant of a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a8e17a0-daa3-5ce7-9699-c81f32093d54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474502} {:answer/id #uuid "dcdf29b7-66c8-593e-b845-6514eb9e9b6d" :answer/